# The folder above ignores every PNG and names its exceptions one at a time,
# because the default capture writes frames nobody asked for and an untracked
# file there fails the gate's tree-clean step for a reason that has nothing to
# do with the push. The same rule applies here.
#
# BOTH FRAMINGS ARE COMMITTED, because they answer different questions. The
# wide frame is the one the game is played at and the one that decides; a
# beside prop moving one tile changes about a tenth of one per cent of it. The
# close frame (-Distance 22) is where the prop is legible, and on this board it
# works: the three clusters stand mid-board, inside the crop. README.md carries
# the measurement.
#
# What is here is the baseline -- the free-neighbour rule as built, on the
# board that makes it act. The candidate frames #282 drew were never tracked
# (this file did not exist, and the folder above's rule caught them); the
# record of what the alternatives looked like is PR #293's description and the
# README's measurements.
#
#   ./tools/capture-beside-props.ps1                   redraws everything
!played/wide/*.png
!played/close/*.png
